.TH WHELK 1 "August 2026" "whelk 0.2.1" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
whelk \- a Unix shell built from first principles
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B whelk
.RI [ --benchmark | --help | --version ]
.br
.B whelk
.RI < " script"
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B whelk
is a Unix shell whose purpose is to make the operating system's process model
visible rather than hide it. It reads commands from standard input, one per
line, and runs them: pipelines, redirection, parameter expansion, quoting, and
job control.
.PP
When standard input is a terminal it prompts, edits the line, keeps history in
.IR ~/.whelk_history ,
and completes commands, paths and variables with the tab key. When standard
input is a pipe or a file it prints no prompt and runs what it is given.
.PP
There is no run-this-file form: commands come from standard input.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.B \-\-benchmark
Measure startup, a single command, and a pipeline on this machine.
.TP
.B \-h, \-\-help
Print usage, including the list of syntax that is deliberately unsupported.
.TP
.B \-V, \-\-version
Print the version.
.SH BUILTINS
.TP
.BI cd " [dir|-|~]"
Change directory;
.B -
returns to the previous one.
.TP
.BI exit " [status]"
Leave, defaulting to the last command's status.
.TP
.B jobs
List jobs with their state.
.TP
.BI fg " [%n]"
Resume a job in the foreground.
.TP
.BI bg " [%n]"
Resume a job in the background.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I ~/.whelkrc
Read before the first prompt when interactive, one command per line.
.TP
.I ~/.whelk_history
Command history, carried between sessions.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B WHELK_TRACE
When set to 1, print timings for parsing, expansion, resolution and spawning.
.SH EXIT STATUS
The status of the last command run; 127 if a command was not found, 126 if it
was found but not executable, 128 plus the signal number if it was killed by a
signal, and 2 for an error on whelk's own command line.
.SH LIMITATIONS
.B whelk
deliberately does not implement
.BR && ", " || ", " ; ,
globbing, command substitution, here-documents, shell functions, aliases, or
multi-line input. These are parsed and refused by name rather than mistaken for
arguments.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR dash (1),
.BR bash (1)
.SH AUTHOR
whelk contributors. Source at https://github.com/ferris007/whelk
